pf":[],"description_full":"Armstrong World Industries, Inc., together with its subsidiaries, engages in the design, manufacture, and sale of ceiling and wall solutions in the Americas. It operates through Mineral Fiber and Architectural Specialties segments. The company offers mineral fiber, fiberglass, metal, wood, felt, architectural resin and glass, wood fiber, and glass-reinforced-gypsum; and ceiling component products, such as ceiling perimeters and trims, as well as grid products that support drywall ceiling systems. It also designs, produces, and sources specialty ceilings, walls, and other interior and exterior architectural applications primarily for use in commercial settings; and manufactures ceiling suspension system (grid) products. It serves commercial and residential construction markets, as well as renovation of existing buildings sectors. The company sells its products to resale distributors, ceiling system contractors, wholesalers, and retailers comprising large home centers. Armstrong World Industries, Inc. was founded in 1860 and is headquartered in Lancaster, Pennsylvania.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Mark A. Hershey J.D. | President, CEO & Director | Age 55 | Compensation: $1.2M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 4K people. Revenue per employee of $395K ranks in the above the sector median among Industrials peers. Each employee generates $60K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $5K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 3\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (1/10).","financial_summary":["Armstrong World Industries Inc is a mid-cap industrials business with a $7.4B market cap on $1.6B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +10% over the past three years and -4% annually across the full decade, a respectable pace for a business of this size.","Returns on capital run at 22% against a 9% cost of capital \u2014 a 13-point spread that is the clearest signature of an economic moat, with gross margins sitting at 36%.","The business throws off $246M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 15 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.15\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 0.5\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and interest coverage of 13\u00d7 is fortress-like.","Shareholder yield of 2.5% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome, and within its sector the business captures 2.4\u00d7 its fair share of profits, a structural advantage visible in the numbers."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Price sits 34% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion against estimate error has been bid away","sev":"red","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.3354990797803731},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Industrials sector lagging the market significantly, underperforming by 6% over 3 months","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Cyclicality and capital intensity \u2014 order books turn on a dime, and the heavy fixed cost base that creates the moat also magnifies the downturn.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Supply chain reshoring costs and labour shortages \u2014 onshoring mandates raise capex requirements while a shrinking skilled-labour pool inflates operating costs.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Tariff and trade-policy exposure \u2014 industrial exporters sit squarely in the crosshairs of protectionist policy, and retaliatory tariffs can close markets without warning.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Operating margins 18% above sector median \u2014 the quantitative trace of pricing power","Piotroski score 9/9 \u2014 financials improving across profitability, leverage and efficiency","Altman Z comfortably in the safe zone \u2014 a fortress balance sheet on this measure","Healthy payout ratio leaves room for both dividend growth and reinvestment","ROIC sits in the top quartile of Industrials peers \u2014 the moat is wider than the neighbourhood","Financial quality scores above most Industrials peers"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Critical Analysis: AAON (NASDAQ:AAON) & Armstrong World Industries (NYSE:AWI)","source":"thelincolnianonline.com","link":"https://www.thelincolnianonline.com/2026/11/13/critical-analysis-aaon-nasdaqaaon-armstrong-world-industries-nyseawi.html","date":"2026-11-13","timestamp":1794572328.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.876,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Trane Technologies (NYSE:TT) & Armstrong World Industries (NYSE:AWI) Critical Contrast","source":"thelincolnianonline.com","link":"https://www.thelincolnianonline.com/2026/11/11/trane-technologies-nysett-armstrong-world-industries-nyseawi-critical-contrast.html","date":"2026-11-11","timestamp":1794421734.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.876,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Discipline and Rules-Based Execution in AWI Response","source":"news.stocktradersdaily.com","link":"https://news.stocktradersdaily.com/news_release/35/Discipline_and_Rules-Based_Execution_in_AWI_Response_111026124201_1794289321.html","date":"2026-11-10","timestamp":1794289321.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.0,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Armstrong World Industries Shares Updated Investor Presentation Ahead of Baird Global Industrial Conference","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/us/news/announcements/armstrong-world-industries-shares-updated-inves